# URL of the Keystone app (project management), used by "Ouvrir dans Keystone".
NEXT_PUBLIC_KEYSTONE_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Where Codex keeps its local database and uploaded files (default: ./.data).
# CODEX_DATA_DIR=.data

# ── Integration API (docs/api.md) ──
# Bearer token of POST /api/import (migration tool). Unset: the import API is disabled (503).
# CODEX_IMPORT_TOKEN=change-me
# Maximum size of an imported file, in MB (default 100).
# CODEX_IMPORT_MAX_MB=100
# Bearer token of POST /api/integrations/vigie (Vigie decisions webhook). Unset: the webhook is disabled (503).
# VIGIE_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=change-me
# Vigie API; while unset, Vigie decisions can be simulated from Codex by a records manager.
# VIGIE_API_URL=http://localhost:3200/api
# URL of the Vigie app, used by "Ouvrir dans Vigie".
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VIGIE_URL=http://localhost:3200

# ── File storage (src/lib/storage.ts) ──
# local (default): files under $CODEX_DATA_DIR/files. sharepoint: a SharePoint library driven by Codex through
# Microsoft Graph (app-only); only the file, its name and its version go to SharePoint, all metadata stays in Codex.
# CODEX_STORAGE=local
# CODEX_SP_TENANT_ID=
# CODEX_SP_CLIENT_ID=
# CODEX_SP_CLIENT_SECRET=
# CODEX_SP_DRIVE_ID=

# ── HTTP security headers (src/lib/security-headers.ts, src/proxy.ts) ──
# Set to 1 only when Codex is served over HTTPS (behind a TLS reverse proxy): adds Strict-Transport-Security
# (one year, this host only) and upgrade-insecure-requests. Leave unset locally and in the Mac app (plain HTTP):
# HSTS on localhost would force HTTPS on every local app.
# CODEX_HTTPS=1

# ── Rate limiting (src/lib/ratelimit.ts) ──
# Requests allowed per key, as "N" (per minute) or "N/<duration>" (30s, 1m, 15m, 1h); "0" or "off" lifts a limit.
# Beyond, 429 with Retry-After. Counters live in memory, in the one server process (see README).
# Set to 1 only when a reverse proxy in front of Codex writes X-Forwarded-For (replaces it, or appends the address it
# sees) and clients cannot reach Codex without it. Unset (default, local use, Mac app): the client may write that header
# itself, so its address never holds anyone else back: the right token always goes through, the registry is not
# limited and the browser routes count per user only.
# CODEX_TRUST_PROXY=1
# Lifts every limit below:
# CODEX_RATE_LIMIT=off
# POST /api/import and POST /api/integrations/vigie, per client address, counted once the token is checked:
# CODEX_RATE_IMPORT=600/1m
# CODEX_RATE_VIGIE=600/1m
# Tokens refused on those two, per client address: beyond, wrong tokens get 429 instead of 401; with
# CODEX_TRUST_PROXY=1, the address then waits even with the right token:
# CODEX_RATE_TOKEN_FAILURES=20/15m
# GET /api/registry, per client address, with CODEX_TRUST_PROXY=1 only:
# CODEX_RATE_REGISTRY=300/1m
# Stored files and approval records, then CSV exports, per user (as resolved from the session, not the raw cookie),
# and per client address too with CODEX_TRUST_PROXY=1:
# CODEX_RATE_FILES=600/1m
# CODEX_RATE_EXPORTS=30/1m
# Server Actions that write, per user:
# CODEX_RATE_WRITES=120/1m
